<!--Post: 10066--><!--Time: 1109739718-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10066"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ozdude<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ozdude<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 9:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t know about the contents...I don&#39;t know if this is a mistake and should go under another thread, but KAKE &#40;I don&#39;t even like that station, so I hate to keep using them&#41; said police got the church name off the computer disk, got a list of people with access from the church, had Dennis driving around the Home Depot several times and dumping the package, then zeroing in on the license plate, ran it for the name, and that is how the cow chips fell where they did...didn&#39;t someone post one time that with all the cameras in public places and computer technology that would do him in..both apparently did.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10066-->
<!--Post: 10077--><!--Time: 1109741421-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10077"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 9:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Back to the initial question of this thread, What is the biggest mistake LE made?  <BR>Assuming that serial killers do not evolve!  <BR>That after the 1974 and 1977 murders and his techniques and communications and methods of operation that any other unsolved murder of women in Wichita was not him because they didn&#39;t quite match up or fit in or were out of the central crime area of the 70&#39;s murders or were unclaimed by him. They didn&#39;t look beyond that.  <BR>They didn&#39;t hear from him and thought he was dead or incarerated. <BR> He was there all along commiting at least five more murders beyond the seven in the 1970&#39;s. 10 that we know of now linked to him. Into the 90&#39;s and beyond.  <BR>even when he comes back with his communications and sends proof of another murder in the 1980&#39;s does this send up a red flag that there could be more out there? <BR>Apparently not. Unless he admits to it or sends in some proof why look into any of the dozens of unsolved murders that could be attributed to him? If he doesn&#39;t claim it he probably didn&#39;t do it so why waste time looking into any of those cases. <BR>This all smiles-pat ourselves on the back-hugs all around and high fives for all press conference came a little late. It should have been held years ago. <BR>More appropriate announcement of the the capture of the BTK suspect would have been a somber one saying we finally got the guy. We didn&#39;t know enough about serial killers. We went with what we knew or what we thought. We should have went farther and looked deeper. We have a message for any community past present or future that has a serial killer uncaptured, don&#39;t assume anything, expect the unexepected, investigate, ask for additional resources and if you don&#39;t get them ask again and if you still don&#39;t get them improvise. Don&#39;t be afraid to make some mistakes along the way. Look back, look ahead and don&#39;t blink.    <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10077-->
<!--Post: 10101--><!--Time: 1109755464-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10101"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 1:24 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You summed it up with &#34;didn&#39;t look beyond that&#34;. If they thought he was dead or incarcerated that should have narrowed the search considerably. Plus it would have made him a non-moving target. I think that the fact that none of the momentos showed up in a year or two should have been a hint that he was still around. And the fact that bodies kept showing up with no suspects was a good clue. one of the things I want to do is go over all the clues he sent and parse it out into statements to see what percentage were accurate. Just because a serial killer says he will not change his M.O., should you believe him?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10101-->
<!--Post: 10124--><!--Time: 1109777426-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10124"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 7:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I disagree, I know that after Marine was found in&#40;1985&#41; some of my family members were questioned for 2 hours. It was only recently that I figured out why. The investigator&#39;s were still trying to make a Coleman connection in 1985 based on the fact that Mrs. Otero, Ms. Bright and her brother were employees there in 1973 or 1974! When investigator&#39;s realized that some of my family worked at Coleman during that time period,they were questioned extensivley. They were still trying to make that connection 10 years later!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
